WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
Where did we get this idea that&nbsp;
light-to-moderate drinking was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.334
beneficial for health? Are there&nbsp;
any benefits? Watch to find out.

00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:15.602
"Friday Favorites: Is It Better to Drink Little Alcohol Than None?
Do Any Benefits Outweigh Risks?"

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.080
Why do we not see the corporate
interests of the alcohol industry

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.440
as clearly as we see those
of the tobacco industry?

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.680
Well, the alcohol industry has waged
a sophisticated and successful campaign

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.040
over the last few decades, undermining
perceptions of the extent

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.560
of alcohol-related harm to health

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.440
by framing the argument as
a balance of benefits and harms.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.440
Yes, alcohol may be an intoxicating
carcinogen, increasing cancer risk,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.840
but what about reducing
heart disease risk?

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.600
Policymakers hesitate to introduce
effective alcohol policies,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.000
or even to support the
addition of warning labels

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.840
for fear they might undermine or contradict
any possible health benefits of alcohol use.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.400
After all, alcohol consumption clearly raises
HDL, the supposed "good" cholesterol.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.760
But sadly, as I've already explored, HDL
is no longer considered protective,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.880
based in part on so-called Mendelian
randomization studies,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.600
where having a high HDL your whole
life doesn't appear to help,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.280
whereas a lifelong reduction
of bad cholesterol, LDL,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:26.160
just thanks to luck-of-the-draw genetics,
does indeed decrease heart disease risk.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.040
So the boost in HDL from
alcohol may not matter,

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.720
and if you look at subclinical
markers of atherosclerosis,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.680
like the thickening of the wall of
your carotid arteries in your neck,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.560
those that abstain from alcohol completely
seem to be at the lowest risk,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.520
and the same with coronary
calcium scores, where, in general,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.000
the lower the alcohol consumption,
the lower the risk.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.760
And alcohol bumps our blood
pressure up a bit as well,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.240
which would be expected to
raise, not lower, our cardiac risk.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.320
So where do we get this idea
that alcohol was good for us?

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.440
From the famous J curve.
Check it out.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.800
If you follow large populations
of people over time, in general

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.160
the more people drink, the higher
their risk of dying prematurely,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.520
but the lowest risk, those
who tend to live the longest,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.320
were not those who drank
zero, the abstainers,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.120
but those who drank moderately,
like one drink a day.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.600
That's why you get some folks recommending
that physicians should counsel

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.720
lifelong nondrinkers to take up the habit.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.000
Sure, there are statin drugs, but alcoholic
beverages don't require a prescription,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.800
are far cheaper, and
certainly more enjoyable.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.640
Is moderate drinking really protective,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.600
or is there just something about people
who abstain completely from alcohol

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.280
that puts them in a higher risk category?

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.560
The reason we suspect
something fishy is going on

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.080
is that abstainers seem to be at higher
risk of a whole swath of diseases

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.760
including, ironically, liver cirrhosis.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.440
Compared to lifelong abstainers,
those who've never touched the stuff,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.480
men and women drinking a little
appear to have less liver cirrhosis.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:02.480
Wait---what?

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.480
How could a little drinking be linked
to lower rates of liver cirrhosis?

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.400
Well, let's think about it.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.080
What makes more sense,

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.800
that drinking lead to less liver cirrhosis
or liver cirrhosis lead to less drinking?

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.960
In other words, reverse causation,
the so-called sick quitter effect.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.400
If you look at studies of smokers,
sometimes you see higher mortality rates

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.160
among those who quit smoking compared
to those that continue smoking.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.960
Why? Because the reason they quit
smoking is because they got sick.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.840
So, of course, sick people die
more often than less sick people.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.000
That's why when you classify someone
as a non-smoker in a study,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.160
you have to make sure
they're a lifelong non-smoker

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.120
and not just a non-smoker---
since last Tuesday.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.760
Yet, unbelievably, that's not what
they do in most alcohol studies,

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.560
where instead they misclassify former
drinkers as if they were lifelong abstainers.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.800
And, look, individuals with poorer
health are more likely to cut down

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.640
or stop drinking completely, thereby
making current drinkers look good

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.800
in comparison to those who drink zero,
because some of the abstainers

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.160
are just abstaining because
they got sick and stopped.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.520
OK. So, what if you went
back to all those studies

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.520
and corrected the misclassifications,

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.520
separate out the former drinkers
from the lifelong abstainers?

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.200
We didn't know until now.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.480
They, indeed, found drinker
misclassification errors all too common,

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.800
plaguing three quarters of the studies,

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.000
and when they controlled for that
the J-shaped curve disappeared.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.840
The death versus alcohol relationship
became more consistent

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.560
with a straight line, linear dose response,
meaning more alcohol, more death:

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.760
no protection at low
levels of consumption.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.120
So no apparent benefit of light
to moderate drinking after all,

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.200
when you use better comparison groups.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.760
Although these results are not
what the majority of drinking

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.520
adults may desire to believe,
the public deserves to hear

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.160
and to read in more complete
and balanced detail

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:07.120
the ever-growing evidence

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.541
that drinking alcohol is very
unlikely to improve their health.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.120
Once you remove from studies
on alcohol and mortality

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.640
the systematic error of misclassifying former
drinkers as if they were lifelong abstainers,

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.920
moderate alcohol consumption,
like a glass of wine a day,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.240
does not appear to be protective after all.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.080
The immediate implication
from this new research

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.600
is that clinicians need to be highly
skeptical about the hypothesized

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.120
health benefits of alcohol consumption

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.280
and should not advise their patients
to drink to improve their life expectancy.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.800
This is especially important given
increasing awareness of cancer risks

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.280
from even moderate alcohol use.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.600
Given the cancer risk, if there's
just harms and no benefits,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.960
then the ideal alcohol intake on a routine
day-to-day basis should really be zero,

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.960
potentially making it a red-light beverage.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.120
The problem was that many
of these population studies

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.440
classified those that quit drinking in
response to ill-health as nondrinkers.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.440
This is the problem of reverse causation:

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.280
instead of abstaining leading to poor health,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.280
poor health may have lead to abstaining.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.080
It's like when studies show those who sit
around and watch TV have worse health.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.040
Is more TV leading to illness,

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.960
or is illness leading to
more TV on the couch?

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:33.520
That's one of the reasons why if you
look at the hierarchy of evidence,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.240
where higher on the pyramid
means stronger evidence,

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.640
interventional trials, like
randomized controlled trials,

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.040
tend to offer better evidence than
observational studies of populations,

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.440
which can suffer from both reverse
causation and confounding factors.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.160
For example, light drinkers as a group may
be more likely to drink their glass of wine

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.280
with a salad than a cheeseburger, and
that's why the wine appeared protective.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.040
But sometimes it's hard to do
randomized controlled trials,

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:04.720
like you can't randomize people to
smoke a pack a day for a few decades.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.320
So sometimes you have to base your
decisions on observational studies.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.520
But now we have a new tool,
Mendelian randomization.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.400
In cases where randomized controlled
trials are not feasible or practical,

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.640
this new tool can provide reliable evidence
on the cause and effect relationship

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:25.440
between exposures and risks of disease.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.920
It's like the HDL story.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.880
Alcohol does raise your HDL
'good' cholesterol levels.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.640
But unfortunately, it seems

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.120
good cholesterol isn't any good

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.680
at lowering heart disease risk after all,

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.080
based in part on Mendelian
randomization studies,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.080
where people who were randomly assigned

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:48.080
higher HDL levels genetically from
birth don't appear to be protected.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:50.160
Is there any way to study people

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.560
who are randomly assigned since conception

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:53.920
to not drink as much?

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.840
Remarkably, yes.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:03.360
Alcohol is detoxified in the liver to carbon
dioxide and water by two enzymes.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.120
But in the process, a toxic
intermediate metabolite is produced

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:11.600
called acetaldehyde, which can cause
unpleasant nausea and flushing sensations.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.480
So if people are born with a
slow variant of this enzyme,

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.840
or a superfast variant of this enzyme,
acetaldehyde can build up,

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.760
making alcohol drinking in these people
a relatively unpleasant experience

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:26.640
throughout their lives.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.800
So they're just born less likely to drink as much.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.560
So do they have an increased
risk of heart disease,

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:37.040
like the original observational
studies would suggest?

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.920
No, they have reduced
risk of heart disease.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.400
This suggests that reduction
of alcohol consumption,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.280
even for light to moderate drinkers,
is beneficial for cardiovascular health.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.480
So this just sheds further doubt
on the protective association

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.440
between 'moderate' alcohol
consumption and heart disease,

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:00.320
which was already plagued
with the confounding and bias.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.720
And now the scientific pillars on which
it's based appear increasingly shaky,

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.560
leading some to suggest the leaning
tower of presumed health benefits

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.160
from 'moderate' alcohol
use has finally collapsed.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.120
Given the harms attributed
to alcohol use,

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.920
it is not surprising that reports suggesting
benefits attracted enthusiasm

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.840
among consumers, the media,
and, of course, the alcohol industry,

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.760
but these apparent benefits
are now evaporating.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:32.640
What conclusions should we draw
from this emerging evidence?

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.840
First, in health as elsewhere, if
something looks too good to be true

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:39.040
—like butter is back—

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.840
it should be treated with great caution.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:45.440
Secondly, health professionals
should discourage drinking.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.680
Thirdly, health advice should
come from health authorities,

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.240
not from the alcohol industry, which
should remove all misleading references

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.760
to purported health benefits, which
are increasingly looking more

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.000
like a triumph of spin doctoring
than good science,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.920
as contrived as the alleged split
among scientists over climate change,

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.880
advanced by the petroleum industry.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:10.160
As an intoxicating, addictive,
toxic, carcinogenic drug,

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:14.080
alcohol is not a great choice as a
therapeutic agent, even if it did help.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.040
There are better ways
to prevent heart attacks,

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.040
namely diet and exercise,
and drugs when necessary.

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:23.600
In contrast to that of alcohol,

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.200
effectiveness of lifestyle interventions
has been demonstrated,

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:30.160
and as a bonus has no abuse potential.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:36.448
There's a reason there's
no Appleholics Anonymous.

